## Likely isn't the same as true

<At levels="simple">

Ask GPT-2 who first walked on the moon and it writes that it was "a man named John, who was born
in 1829." This is called <GlossaryPopover term="hallucination">hallucination</GlossaryPopover>.<Cite id="ji2023" />
The model learned to write text that looks like its training text, and nothing checks whether it's
true.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Ask GPT-2 who first walked on the moon and it writes that it was "a man named John, who was born in
1829." Ask about a painter who doesn't exist, and it describes his famous painting. This is called <GlossaryPopover term="hallucination">hallucination</GlossaryPopover>.<Cite id="ji2023" /> The model
was trained to produce text that *looks like* its training text, and a confident sentence about a
painter looks like many real ones. Nothing in the loop from <ChapterRef to="generating-text" /> checks whether it's true.

</At>

<At levels="simple">

Tuning helps, but it never removes the problem completely. That's one reason assistants are given
tools to look things up.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Tuning helps: in the InstructGPT tests, the tuned models made up information about half as often as
GPT-3, 21% of the time instead of 41%.<Cite id="ouyang2022" /> But the cause is the same, so it never
goes away completely. That's one reason assistants are given tools to look things up. Ways to catch
a guess come in <ChapterRef to="making-things-up" />.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### Why training rewards a good guess

Training lowers the cross-entropy loss from <ChapterRef to="learning-from-mistakes" />, the average
of $-\log p$ over the real next tokens. That rewards giving probability to whatever text tends to
come next, whether it's true or not: after "a man named", a plausible first name earns some
probability, and the loss has no term for being wrong about the world. Researchers separate
*intrinsic* hallucinations, which contradict text the model was given, from *extrinsic* ones,
which can't be checked against it at all.<Cite id="ji2023" />

</At>

## Frozen in time

<At levels="simple">

GPT-2 learned from web pages from before 2018, so its guesses for the latest iPhone are the iPhone
6, 5, and 7.<Cite id="radford2019" /> Every model has a cutoff like this, and knows nothing about
later events unless they're put into its prompt.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

GPT-2's training text only includes web links from before 2018.<Cite id="radford2019" /> Ask it
for the latest iPhone and its top guesses are the iPhone 6, 5, and 7, the ones that filled the pages
it read. Every model has a cutoff like this: it knows nothing about later events unless they're put
into its prompt.

</At>

## Remembered word for word

<At levels="simple">

A model mostly learns patterns, but it can also repeat some of its training text word for word.
Researchers got GPT-2 to repeat hundreds of passages exactly, including names and phone numbers
that had been posted online.<Cite id="carlini2021" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

A model mostly learns patterns, but it also keeps some text word for word. By prompting GPT-2 and
sorting through what it wrote, researchers pulled out hundreds of passages it had memorized exactly:
names, phone numbers, and email addresses that had been posted online, chat logs, and code. Some
appeared in just one document of its training text, and larger models memorized more.<Cite id="carlini2021" />
So a model can repeat private or copyrighted text it was trained on, word for word.

</At>

## Learned from people's text

<At levels="simple">

A model also learns the habits of the people who wrote its training text, stereotypes included.
In the lab, after "The nurse said that", GPT-2 rates " she" about five times as likely as " he".
After "The engineer said that", it rates " he" more than eight times as likely as " she". Tuned
models learn not to say stereotypes outright, but tests that look for them indirectly still find
them.<Cite id="bai2024" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Training text is written by people, so a model learns their associations along with their facts,
stereotypes included. In the lab, after "The nurse said that", GPT-2 gives " she" 14.9% and " he"
2.9%. After "The engineer said that", it gives " he" 6.9% and " she" 0.8%. Nothing in the
prompt says who the nurse or the engineer is; the gap comes from the text it read. Tuning teaches
assistants not to state stereotypes, but that doesn't remove the associations: eight models that
pass standard bias benchmarks still showed 21 stereotypes across race, gender, religion, and
health when tested indirectly. The
associations also showed up in their choices, such as which of two people to pick for a
task.<Cite id="bai2024" />

</At>

## Tokens, not letters

Ask a model how many r's are in "strawberry" and it may get it wrong, because it never sees the
letters:

<SpellingBlindSpot />

<At levels="simple">

Numbers have the same problem. In the lab, after "2 + 2 =", GPT-2 rates " 3" more likely than
" 4".

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Numbers have the same problem: they are split into tokens in uneven ways, so the model can't simply
line up the digits and carry, the way you would on paper. In the lab, after "2 + 2 =", GPT-2 rates
" 3" more likely than " 4".

</At>
<At levels="deep">

GPT-2's tokens split numbers wherever its merges happened to land: " 2023" is " 20" + "23", and
" 1234567" is " 123" + "45" + "67", so the same digit can sit in a different place within a
token from one number to the next. GPT-4's tokenizer cuts numbers into groups of up to three
digits from the left instead: "202" + "3", and "123" + "456" + "7". That's tidier, but the groups
still don't line up with the ones, tens, and hundreds you'd add column by column.

</At>

## Wording matters

<At levels="simple">

Asking the same thing another way can change the answer. "The capital of France is" makes GPT-2
ramble; "Q: What is the capital of France? A:" gets "Paris". Much larger models swing with small
formatting changes too.<Cite id="sclar2024" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

"The capital of France is" and "Q: What is the capital of France? A:" ask the same thing, but they
look like different kinds of text. In the lab, the first makes GPT-2 ramble about "the capital of
the French Republic"; the second gets "Paris" in its first sentence. Much larger models can also
swing with small formatting changes: one study found accuracy differences of up to 76 percentage
points from formatting alone.<Cite id="sclar2024" />

</At>

## Limited memory and loops

<At levels="simple">

A model only sees its context window: for GPT-2, 1,024 tokens. Anything earlier is gone. And
always taking the most likely token can trap a model in a loop.<Cite id="holtzman2020" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

A model only sees its context window (<ChapterRef to="the-context-problem" />): for GPT-2, 1,024 tokens. Anything earlier is cut off
and simply gone. And as <ChapterRef to="generating-text" /> showed, always taking the most likely token can trap a model in a
loop, like the cat that sits on the bed forever.<Cite id="holtzman2020" />

</At>

<RecapCard>

- Models produce likely text, not checked facts, so they can confidently make things up.
- They know nothing after their training cutoff unless told.
- They can repeat some of their training text word for word.
- They learn the stereotypes in their training text, and tuning hides them more than it removes
  them.
- They read tokens, not letters or digits, which makes spelling and arithmetic hard.
- Small changes in wording can change the answer, and anything outside the context window is
  forgotten.

<At levels="deep">

- The loss rewards likely text, not true text; GPT-2's number tokens don't line up with digit
  columns.

</At>
</RecapCard>

**Next question:** several of these quirks come from answering straight away. What is a model doing
when it pauses to "think" first?
